      "data": {
        "id": "400c4524-d52e-818e-81ca-e64fdb03aab0",
        "markdown": "Product Hunt day: 2026-10-19\nSource: https://www.producthunt.com/leaderboard/daily/2026/10/19/all\nCaptured: 2026-10-21T00:33:05.555Z\nLocal file: archive/2026-10-19__2026-10-21T00-33-05.555Z.json\nSHA-256: ee1fe1b06bbbb6b1a1a1877f6a8c56776149a1b128b2f70e4203310c1b440d63\nProducts: 867\n## Synthesis: Product Hunt 2026-10-19\n867 launches were archived (1,040 feed entries, minus 173 ads). Attention was very concentrated: only 28 launches reached 50+ votes, and 761 finished with 2 votes or fewer. 342 launches (39%) ship real AI by the archive's strict standard, where marketing \"AI\" with nothing concrete does not count. The day's theme was tooling for people who run coding agents: 55 launches are built around Claude Code, Codex, Cursor or similar agents, 50 speak MCP, and 59 are macOS-native.\n## Notable launches\n#1 Beam.win (363 votes): streams individual app windows from an agent-running Mac to another Mac as native windows. https://beam.win/ · https://www.producthunt.com/posts/beam-win\n#2 Figma Specs (212): collaborative Markdown specs on the Figma canvas that pass design context to agents via MCP. https://www.figma.com/specs/ · https://www.producthunt.com/posts/figma-specs\n#3 readywhen (191): an AI personal assistant that lives inside WhatsApp with its own number, voice notes and calls. https://app.readywhen.ai/start · https://www.producthunt.com/posts/readywhen-2\n#4 walkai-talkai (161): push-to-talk from a handheld radio to coding agents, using local Whisper and CoreML text-to-speech. https://walkai-talkai.com/ · https://www.producthunt.com/posts/walkai-talkai\n#5 Kai Resolve by Gleap (148): a second-level support agent that investigates tickets using screen recordings, logs and read-only code access. https://www.gleap.ai/kai-resolve/ · https://www.producthunt.com/posts/kai-resolve-by-gleap\n#7 Beam by Reflection (132): an open-weight sparse mixture-of-experts LLM (501B total, 23B active parameters) for coding and agentic work. https://reflection.ai/beam · https://www.producthunt.com/posts/beam-13\n#8 buddylist (123): a retro MSN-style buddy list where Claude Code, Codex, Gemini and OpenCode appear as chat contacts; open source. https://buddylist.dev/ · https://www.producthunt.com/posts/buddylist-2\n#9 Framefields (99): an open-source TypeScript video renderer on WebGPU in Node, with no headless browser. https://framefields.dev/ · https://www.producthunt.com/posts/framefields\n#10 Lazy AWS (98): an open-source keyboard-driven terminal UI for AWS that previews every action as the exact CLI command. https://lazyaws.xyz/ · https://www.producthunt.com/posts/lazy-aws\nAlso notable:<br>- Quello (87): cited answers across connected tools. https://quello.dev/<br>- Lookout for Slack (83): plain-language urgent-message triggers. https://slacklookout.com/<br>- Tori (81): a RAG support agent from TalkJS. https://asktori.ai/<br>- Own 1 (80): a \\$3,899 local-LLM desktop. https://www.producthunt.com/posts/own-1<br>- Lancetta (76): advises when to push or ease off based on Claude Code and Codex quota usage. https://lancetta.app/<br>- Point and Yap (73): point at the screen and talk to agents via MCP. https://pointandyap.com/\n## Patterns: AI and developer tools\nAgent operations is now its own category. Several launches don't build agents at all; they help people run them. That covers remote windows for agent Macs (Beam.win), voice and radio input (walkai-talkai, Point and Yap), quota pacing (Lancetta) and multi-agent chat front-ends (buddylist). MCP has become a default integration surface (50 launches), from Figma Specs and Quello to small MCP servers for crawling and review.\nSupport is the most-voted applied-AI vertical (Kai Resolve, Tori, Lookout). The pitch has moved from deflecting tickets to investigating them with logs, screen recordings and code access.\nLocal-first and private AI keeps recurring: 91 launches stress on-device or local processing, from Own 1's local-LLM hardware to on-device speech-to-text and vision. Open source is a frequent trust signal (73 launches).\nBelow the top 30, the long tail is dominated by single-purpose utilities (QR codes, PDF tools, habit and focus timers, trackers) and AI image or video generators with thin differentiation.\n## Counts and caveats\n- Websites: 14 listed websites failed to open; those products are summarized from Product Hunt data only. The failures were DNS errors including a misspelled domain, 404s, a 503, blank JS pages, a Streamlit auth loop, a bot-verification interstitial and a Cloudflare challenge.<br>- Not-genuine links: 19 listings have no genuine official website. 18 gift-card giveaway or credit-score \"guide\" posts use rb.gy shorteners that lead to one giveaway Blogspot post, and a \"Gift card\" listing redirects through asiafilm.org to an XM forex promotion.<br>- Spam: 42 listings carry spam signals, including SEO pages for local businesses, modded-APK promotion, prescription-drug and opioid sale pages, get-paid-to and guaranteed-return schemes, and piracy or scanlation sites. All are kept.<br>- Duplicate product page: two distinct YBK posts share one product page; both are kept.<br>- Frozen data: vote counts and ranks come from the frozen list captured 2026-10-21T00:33:05.555Z, after the day closed.\n<file src=\"https://prod-files-secure.s3.us-west-2.amazonaws.com/e11c4524-d52e-81f7-8b52-000383f5a185/3f29730b-838f-4946-b5d5-85a87b9b7c51/2026-10-19__2026-10-21T00-33-05.555Z.json?X-Amz-Algorithm=AWS4-HMAC-SHA256&REDACTED&X-Amz-Credential=ASIAZI2LB4666XPLBPM6%2F20261023%2Fus-west-2%2Fs3%2Faws4_request&X-Amz-Date=20261023T160104Z&X-Amz-Expires=3600&REDACTED%2F%2F%2F%2F%2F%2F%2F%2F%2F%2FwEaCXVzLXdlc3QtMiJIMEYCIQDQVz26ifJQMz%REDACTED%2FCuoOhuKpDUXanKogECJD%2F%2F%2F%2F%2F%2F%2F%2F%2F%REDACTED%2BR%REDACTED%2FGv6g%2FkmY%REDACTED%REDACTED%REDACTED%2FMIZIdoJ%REDACTED%2BND7j0LZ1vbN7suhgEI1%2FVOEuppLvA%2FFDHXJy%REDACTED%2FjPuQImLVFPeHdZmfK62oyB%REDACTED%2BlgchDpfMfE3C2%REDACTED%REDACTED%2B9SlspCLK2u2mxiBJjCZ%2Fu3WB